      <section data-sec="" id="before" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px 44px;">
        <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: var(--vy-ink);">Before: a system with one door into it</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">The old PMS worked, in the sense that the front desk could check a guest in and take a payment. Nobody else could get into it. Housekeeping had a printed sheet, maintenance had a notebook behind the desk, and the only connection between those two and the reservation system was a person walking between them.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">On a forty-room turnover day this produced a specific kind of chaos. Reception could not see which rooms were ready, so they phoned the floor. Every call interrupted somebody cleaning, which made the room later, which produced another call. Guests waited in the lobby with their luggage while two departments who both wanted to help them were unable to see each other's work.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-sec="" id="switch" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px 44px;">
        <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: var(--vy-ink);">The switch: February, and one department at a time</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Seven weeks in the quietest month of the year, and the sequence mattered more than the speed. Housekeeping went first, on its own, for a fortnight before anything else changed: the board on phones, the four states, the old printed sheet still coming out in parallel. By the time it was the front desk's turn, the floor already trusted the thing they were being asked to depend on.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Front desk was the real test, and it was where the fortnight of parallel running earned its cost. Two receptionists worked both systems for a full week, and the exercise surfaced a group-booking pattern the import had flattened into individual reservations. That was fixed before cutover rather than discovered in July.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Channels moved on a Tuesday morning in the seventh week, with fourteen arrivals on the board and both teams watching.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-sec="" id="after" data-nopad="" style="position: relative; overflow: hidden; margin: 0 0 44px; padding: 66px 0 70px; background: linear-gradient(165deg,var(--vy-dark) 0%,#2A211B 52%,#221A15 100%);">
        <div style="position: absolute; top: -120px; left: 8%; width: 400px; height: 400px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--vy-accent-3); filter: blur(90px); opacity: .26; pointer-events: none;"></div>
        <div style="position: relative; max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px;">
          <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: #fff;">After: the loop closed</h2>
          <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: rgba(255,247,240,.78);">Reception can see room state without asking, so the calls stopped. A room marked guest-ready appears at the desk in the same second, which means the guest in the lobby is handed a key by someone who already knew rather than someone who has just been told.</p>
          <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: rgba(255,247,240,.78);">Rooms with a guest waiting pin themselves to the top of the board, which changed the shape of the turnover day more than any single feature. The cleaner is not guessing at priority; the priority is on the screen.</p>
          <p style="margin: 0 0 26px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: rgba(255,247,240,.78);">Maintenance was the quiet win. A cleaner photographs a broken shower screen and raises a ticket from the room in fifteen seconds, and if that ticket takes the room out of service the room stops selling immediately — including on the channels. The category of problem where a known-broken room kept being sold has simply gone.</p>
          <div data-two="" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px;">
            <div style="padding: 22px 22px; border-radius: 18px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.055); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.13);">
              <div data-printcallout="" style="font-family: var(--vy-display); font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--vy-accent-3); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin-bottom: 6px;">Desk-to-floor calls</div>
              <div style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.62; color: rgba(255,247,240,.66);">Effectively eliminated on turnover days. Room state is visible to both.</div>
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 22px 22px; border-radius: 18px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.055); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.13);">
              <div data-printcallout="" style="font-family: var(--vy-display); font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--vy-accent-3); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin-bottom: 6px;">Broken rooms off sale</div>
              <div style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.62; color: rgba(255,247,240,.66);">A maintenance ticket blocks the room across every channel, automatically.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section data-sec="" id="honest" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px 48px;">
        <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: var(--vy-ink);">What was harder than expected</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Two long-serving receptionists genuinely did not want to change, and said so. Both were right that the old system was faster for them personally, because fifteen years of muscle memory is worth a lot. The honest answer was that the hotel was optimising for the whole floor rather than for the desk, and that took a difficult conversation rather than a training session. One of them is now the person who trains new starters.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">The group booking import also had to be redone by hand. Fourteen group reservations that the export represented as blocks came across as individual stays, and rebuilding them correctly took a day nobody had planned for.</p>
